    {
      "name": "lsp",
      "description": "Semantic code intelligence — understands code structure, types, and relationships (unlike text search which only matches strings).\n\n**WHEN TO USE — the one rule**: If the question is about a **named code symbol** / **有名字的代码符号** (function / 函数, class / 类, method / 方法, field / 字段, property / 属性, variable / 变量, type / 类型, interface / 接口, enum / 枚举) — its definition, type, signature, callers, implementers, or containing file — use `lsp`. Use `search_content` / `read_file` **only** for **literal text** / **字面文本** (log messages, comments, config strings, unknown patterns).\n\n**Classify by what the question is about, not by the words used.** The same question in English (\"where is X defined\", \"what type is X\", \"who calls X\", \"what's in this file\") and in Chinese (\"X 定义在哪 / 是在哪定义的 / 从哪来的\", \"X 是什么类型\", \"谁调用了 X\", \"这个文件里有什么\") routes to the same `lsp` operation. Do not try to keyword-match user phrasing — if the object of the question is a named symbol, route to `lsp`, regardless of language, formality, or exact wording.\n\n**Do NOT grep for symbol declarations / 不要用文本搜索找符号声明.** Patterns like `function X`, `const X =`, `class X`, `export ... X` are noisy (matches comments, strings, unrelated files) and slower than a single `workspaceSymbol` call.\n\n## Quick Examples (copy & adapt — replace names with the user's actual symbols)\n\n**Outline a file (structure without reading impl):**\n  operation: \"documentSymbol\", filePath: \"path/to/file.ts\"\n\n**Type / signature / docs of a symbol** (works for functions AND class fields / properties):\n  operation: \"hover\", filePath: \"path/to/file.ts\", scope: \"MyClass.myField\"\n\n**Find where a symbol is defined / 找符号定义在哪 — you only know its NAME** (fastest path, no file needed):\n  operation: \"workspaceSymbol\", query: \"mySymbolName\"\n\n**Jump to definition / 跳转到定义 — you have a file that USES the symbol:**\n  operation: \"goToDefinition\", filePath: \"path/to/caller.ts\", find: \"<|>mySymbolName\"\n\n**Find all usages of a symbol:**\n  operation: \"findReferences\", filePath: \"path/to/file.ts\", scope: \"MyClass\"\n\n**Find implementations of an interface:**\n  operation: \"goToImplementation\", filePath: \"path/to/file.ts\", scope: \"IMyInterface\"\n\n**Find what calls a function (incoming) / what a function calls (outgoing):**\n  operation: \"incomingCalls\", filePath: \"path/to/file.ts\", scope: \"myFunction\"\n\n**Read the full body of one symbol (instead of the whole file):**\n  operation: \"readSymbol\", filePath: \"path/to/file.ts\", scope: \"MyClass.myMethod\"\n\n## How to Position (scope + find)\n\n`scope` tells LSP WHERE to look. Three formats:\n- **Symbol path** (recommended): `scope: \"MyClass.myMember\"` — works for any named symbol: classes, functions, methods, fields, properties, nested members (e.g. `\"A.B.c\"`).\n- Line number: `scope: \"42\"` — when you know the exact line.\n- Line range: `scope: \"10,20\"` — search within a range.\n\n`find` (optional) pinpoints the exact token within scope. Use `<|>` as cursor marker:\n- `find: \"return <|>result\"` → positions cursor at `result`.\n\n**Simple rule**: named symbol → `scope` with symbol path. Specific usage on a known line → `scope` with line number + `find`. Only the name is known (nothing else) → `workspaceSymbol` with `query`.\n\n## Pagination\n\nfindReferences / workspaceSymbol return pages. When `hasMore: true`, pass `nextOffset` as `offset` in the next call.\n\n## Fallback\n\nIf LSP returns empty or errors, fall back to `search_content` immediately. Do NOT retry the same failed call.\n\nFor decision trees, natural-language phrasing tables, and advanced workflows, load the \"lsp-code-analysis\" skill via `use_skill`.",
